# PROPAGATION NOTE: this Operator MOVES REAL MONEY, and it is the only one in the
# fleet that does. Everywhere else the money paths are the SDK's and a local edit
# can at worst misreport them. Here `payouts.py` sends sats over Lightning and
# nothing written afterwards can call them back.
#
# The surface a plausible-looking edit can quietly break is the solvency rule in
# `treasury.py`. `payable = sendable − patron float − unpaid obligations − fee
# reserve`, and every one of those subtractions is load-bearing: dropping the
# patron float pays winners out of other patrons' unspent deposits, dropping the
# obligations term lets two settled matches be paid from the same sats, and
# relaxing `trustworthy` turns an unreadable balance from a refusal into an
# optimistic yes. Each of those reads as a slightly larger number, not as a bug.
#
# The second surface is idempotency. `board_store.claim_payout` takes the claim on
# a primary key BEFORE any sats move, and `payouts.send` calls it in that order on
# purpose. Reordering those two — or treating BTCPay's 202 "under way" as "paid" —
# is how one payment becomes two, and the second one cannot be undone.
#
# Neither may be uncovered, and neither should land without a human reading it.
